<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Debug builds only. This file lives in `app/src/debug/`, is referenced by a manifest that
  lives in `app/src/debug/` too, and neither is compiled into `release` — check the merged
  release manifest, not this comment: `app/build/intermediates/merged_manifests/release/`.
  `local` and `beta` are pointed at this same file by hand in `app/build.gradle.kts`, so what
  is written here is the rule for exactly three builds, all of them signed with the debug key.

  Since Android 7 (API 24) an application ignores every certificate authority the phone's
  owner installed himself unless it says otherwise here. That default is right for a
  shipped app and this file does not change it for one.

  What it buys: PRD_SERVER_SYNC_MCP 16 admits nothing but HTTPS, and `ServerAddresses.parse`
  enforces it before a socket is opened whenever the build says so — `http://` is refused by
  name, with no exception for loopback and none for a private address range. So a Mue Platform
  running on a PC at 192.168.1.100 has to present a real certificate, and no public authority
  will ever issue one for an address on somebody's home WiFi. One way out is a certificate
  authority that exists only on that network (`scripts/dev-tls-cert.ts` at the repository
  root), installed on the phone by hand, and an application build that agrees to look at it.
  The trust anchors below are that way out, and it is still the one `release` has.

  ## `cleartextTrafficPermitted` is now `true`, and this is what changed

  The other way out is not to run TLS at all, and the owner has chosen it for his own server:
  it is on his machine, on his network, with no hosting and no public name to obtain a
  certificate for. He was told what it costs — the bearer token in `sync_state` is readable by
  anything else on that WiFi, which is write access to his health data and to his MCP tools —
  and it is his decision. Nothing in the app argues with it and no screen warns about it.

  It is written here rather than left to the platform default in the other direction, for the
  same reason the `false` before it was stated: from API 28 the default is `false`, this app
  runs from API 26, and a value left unwritten would mean two different things on two phones
  the owner might install the same APK on.

  **`release` is untouched, and that is the point of putting this here rather than in `main`.**
  A publishable build has no `networkSecurityConfig` attribute at all and keeps Android's own
  default, so it cannot leave in clear even by accident. The application-level half of the same
  arrangement is `resValue("bool", "cleartext_server_permitted", …)` in `app/build.gradle.kts`,
  declared `false` in `defaultConfig` and overridden to `true` by these same three build types;
  `ServerAddresses.parse` reads it through `SyncContainer` and refuses `http://` in `release`
  before a socket is opened. The two halves have to move together: permit it here and refuse it
  there and the address is rejected at the keyboard; permit it there and refuse it here and
  OkHttp raises `UnknownServiceException`, which `KtorPairingApi.classify` can only read as an
  `IOException` and report as `Unreachable` — "it did not answer", about a server that did.

  What is *not* widened: a bare `mue.home.arpa` is still completed to `https://`, on every
  build. Only an `http://` somebody typed in full stays one.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
      `InsecureBaseConfiguration` is suppressed because on this file it restates the decision
      above rather than reporting a finding: it says the traffic can be read in transit, which
      is exactly what the owner was told and accepted for a server on his own WiFi. It is not
      suppressed to keep a build green — checked, not assumed: with the attribute removed,
      `lintDebug` still succeeds and `lint-results-debug.xml` gains one `InsecureBaseConfiguration`
      entry at severity Warning. It is suppressed because lint analyses a variant and not a source
      set, so the entry would sit permanently on every build a person reads while being false of
      the only one that ships — which is how a real finding later gets scrolled past, the same
      argument `AcceptsUserCertificates` below is suppressed on.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- The platform store, unchanged: every public authority still applies. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
            <!--
              And, in a debug build, whatever the owner installed under Settings → Security →
              Encryption & credentials → Install a certificate → CA certificate. Nothing is
              trusted that the person holding the phone did not put there himself.

              `AcceptsUserCertificates` is suppressed because its message is, here, false: it
              says "in the release version of your app", and lint says it of every occurrence
              it meets because it analyses a variant and not a source set. This file is in
              `src/debug/`. The release APK's binary manifest carries no `networkSecurityConfig`
              attribute at all — `aapt2 dump xmltree app-release-unsigned.apk` over
              `AndroidManifest.xml` lists eleven attributes on `<application>` and `0x01010527`
              is not among them — and `lintVitalRelease` passes on that variant with nothing to
              say. Left unsuppressed, the warning would be a permanent false alarm on a build
              everyone looks at, which is how a real one later gets scrolled past.
            -->
            <certificates src="user" tools:ignore="AcceptsUserCertificates" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
